<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Holy League (1571)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1571">1571</a>–<a href="/years/1573">1573</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Holy League of 1571 was an alliance against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> formed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v">Pope Pius V</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, Savoy, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights of Malta</a> and other Italian states. Its fleet, commanded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria">Don John of Austria</a>, destroyed the Ottoman navy at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> in October 1571. The alliance soon broke up, and Venice made a separate peace in 1573, ceding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/holy-league-1571.jpg" alt="Holy League (1571)" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rafael Monleón · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Estandarte_Liga_Santa.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Holy League was a military coalition arranged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-v">Pope Pius V</a> and formally opened on 15 May 1571, with the aim of breaking Ottoman control of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Its members were the Papal States, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> under Philip II, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights of Malta</a>, the Grand Duchy of Tuscany with the Order of Saint Stephen under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-i-de-medici">Cosimo I de' Medici</a>, the Duchy of Savoy under Emmanuel Philibert, the Duchy of Urbino under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guidobaldo-ii-della-rovere">Guidobaldo II della Rovere</a> and the Duchy of Parma under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottavio-farnese">Ottavio Farnese</a>.</p><p>The League kept places open for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Portugal, but none joined. The Empire preferred its truce with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, France had an anti-Spanish alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and Portugal had no forces to spare.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Each year the members were to have ready by 1 April 200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a>, 100 other ships, 50,000 infantry and 4,500 cavalry. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria">John of Austria</a>, illegitimate half-brother of Philip II, was supreme commander. The fleet first assembled to help the Venetian defenders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, invaded by Ottoman forces under Lala Mustafa in July 1570, but arrived too late to prevent the island's capture.</p><p>On 7 October 1571 the League won a decisive victory over the Ottoman fleet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> in the Gulf of Patras. It fielded 212 warships carrying 28,500 infantry, most of them Venetian, followed by Spanish and Genoese galleys. The victory confirmed a de facto division of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, the east under Ottoman control and the west under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and their Italian allies. In 1572 the fleet, facing a renewed Ottoman navy of 200 vessels under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uluj-ali">Kılıç Ali Pasha</a>, failed to take the chance of a decisive blow at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-navarino">Navarino</a>, owing to friction among its leaders and the reluctance of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria">John of Austria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Pius V died on 1 May 1572 and the diverging interests of the members began to show. In 1573 the fleet did not sail at all; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-austria">John of Austria</a> instead took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> retook in 1574. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, fearing for its Dalmatian possessions and eager to resume trade with the Ottoman Empire, negotiated alone with the Porte. The League ended with the peace treaty of 7 March 1573, which concluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-venetian-war-1570-1573">War of Cyprus</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Holy League (1571)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Mediterranean, galleys from Spain and its Italian kingdoms fought the Ottomans and Barbary corsairs, and a <b>Holy League</b> fleet under Don John of Austria won the Battle of Lepanto in 1571.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Against the Ottoman Empire it fought a series of wars from 1463, contributed about half the galleys of the <b>Holy League</b> at the Battle of Lepanto in 1571, defended Candia for over twenty years until 1669, and conquered the Morea under Francesco Morosini in 1684–1687.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-venice">Republic of Venice · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Spanish sea power was shown at Lepanto (1571), where Spanish galleys formed a major part of the <b>Holy League</b> fleet that defeated the Ottomans, but the Armada sent against England in 1588 was defeated by English ships and wrecked by storms on the voyage home round Scotland and…</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Galleys of the <b>Holy League</b> under Don John of Austria destroyed most of the Ottoman fleet off western Greece.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-7">7 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
